#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
pid-autotune - a throwaway helper for improving a PidCtrl node's gains
against a *live* aquaPi instance, without ever taking the controller out
of the loop.

Design, in one paragraph: the existing PID stays in full closed-loop
control the entire time - this tool only perturbs the *setpoint* by a
small, safe step and watches how the (already-working) controller
responds, under whichever gains happen to be live at the time. That
response is how a candidate gain set gets judged. A single polling loop
(the Recorder below) is the one thing that talks to the sensor: it logs
every reading AND is the safety backstop - the instant the sensor leaves
[--temp-min, --temp-max], it reverts the node to the last-known-good
("proven") gains and aborts, regardless of what the rest of the script
is doing. Losing sensor visibility for several consecutive polls is
treated the same as an out-of-bounds reading (can't claim "it's safe" if
you can't see the sensor).

This intentionally never does open-loop identification (no disabling the
PID to inject a raw step into the heater output) - that would be the
more "textbook" way to identify a plant model, but it also means nothing
is correcting temperature for however long that takes, which is a worse
risk trade for a tank with live fish than a slightly less rigorous
closed-loop estimate.

Subcommands:
    status    - print current gains + live sensor reading, no changes
    baseline  - step the setpoint up by --step-delta and back down again
                under the CURRENT (assumed-proven) gains, recording each
                phase for a fixed --horizon-minutes, save a report (incl.
                a suggested starting point for a candidate d_fact) to
                pid_autotune_runs/ for `trial` and `recommend` to load
    trial     - apply a candidate gain set, repeat the same up+down test
                with the baseline's horizon, compare (ITAE, overshoot,
                number of swings, residual error per phase), and either
                keep the candidate (if it's a clear improvement) or
                explicitly revert to the baseline's proven gains
    revert    - unconditionally write a given (or a saved baseline's)
                gains back right now, no measurement
    recommend - offline, no login: fit a model of the tank to a saved
                baseline (or trial) run, check it replays that run, then
                simulate read interval x averaging x P x I and recommend
                the combination to confirm with `trial`

`baseline` doubles as the tank profiling run for `recommend`; `trial` can
also try a sensor read interval/averaging together with the gains (and
reverts them together).

Needs only `requests` (already an aquaPi dependency). Meant to run on
the aquaPi host itself (--base-url defaults to localhost:5000), from the
repo root:

    venv/bin/python3 tools/pid-autotune status
    venv/bin/python3 tools/pid-autotune baseline      # at night, ~6h
    venv/bin/python3 tools/pid-autotune recommend     # offline, newest baseline
    venv/bin/python3 tools/pid-autotune trial --p-fact ... --i-fact ...

Runs are saved in pid_autotune_runs/ in the repo. `trial` picks the newest
baseline matching the live gains and sensor settings; each new baseline
moves the ones that no longer match (and their trials) into
pid_autotune_runs/archive_<gains>_<sensor settings>.zip.

You'll be prompted for your own aquaPi login (operator/admin) - this
tool never stores or hardcodes any credential.
"""

